  "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3?$",
  "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932041/assets/e992d80b1245e6fad1c1e2968c396c51ef863d56d36c184592f8025fc450fa99.png)\n\n- Reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932041/assets/aa0778c338e9b57aca82057edc1b984e051d05a3f5413c2b1c70c3874fcf063e.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $3$ units down to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,-4)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932041/assets/25b4ef6f489f19d81b1d54a3240faf5e99f20a9463aa2ce9bd2e39796bf39cb3.png)",
